// GitHub issue #351: the kernel's lock backend for kernel/lib/'s pool
// primitives.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-27). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - The mask is DAIF.I only. FIQ, SError and debug exceptions are not
//   masked, matching enable_irq/disable_irq's own scope. A handler at one
//   of those levels that takes a pool lock deadlocks against interrupted
//   code holding it, and nothing here can tell.
// - The spin is unbounded and unfair. A core that never gets the word is
//   indistinguishable from a core waiting on one that is making progress,
//   so a lost release hangs rather than reporting. See
//   kernel/lib/spinlock.tkb for what the wait does and does not do.
// - Nothing here detects a deadlock. Taking a second pool's lock while
//   holding one is legal and correct as far as this file is concerned;
//   whether the ORDER is consistent across call sites is checked by
//   linux_user/intrusive_pool/pool_lock_check.tkb, in the suite that can
//   afford to look.
//
// This is `spin_lock_irqsave`, and it became one in issue #445 without
// its shape changing -- which is what the previous version of this header
// predicted, because `flags` was already the thing such an API hands back.
// The order is the part that matters: MASK, then take; give back, then
// RESTORE. Taking the lock first would leave a window where this core
// holds it and an interrupt can arrive, and a handler that then wants the
// same lock waits for a holder that cannot run until the handler returns.
//
// The caller keeping `flags` is what makes the pair correct where a bare
// disable/enable is not -- the pool's guard is a linear struct carrying
// it, the frame slot Linux's own `unsigned long flags` occupies:
//
//   in an interrupt handler, DAIF.I is already set on entry, so the
//   acquire records "was masked" and the matching release leaves it
//   masked instead of enabling interrupts inside a handler;
//
//   nested across two pools, the inner acquire records "already masked"
//   and its release does nothing, while the outermost records the truth
//   and restores it.
//
// Storing the saved state globally instead was considered and is racy: a
// thread that reads the mask, takes an interrupt before it can set it,
// and resumes after the handler has overwritten the global would restore
// the handler's value and leave interrupts masked for good. Per-guard
// state has no such window and needs no depth counter.
use "kernel/arch/arm64/boot/cpu.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/mutex.tkb";

// Bit 7 of DAIF is the I (IRQ) mask.
const DAIF_IRQ_BIT: usize = 7;

// GitHub issue #451: the interrupt-mask half of every Mutex in this
// kernel, kept here rather than in kernel/lib/mutex.tkb so that the Mutex
// itself stays host-compilable -- linux_user substitutes this file and
// gives these two nothing to do, which is the same boundary
// pool_lock_acquire already lives on (issue #364), not a second one.
//
// Returns 1 if IRQs were already masked, 0 if they were enabled. The
// caller keeps it, in a linear guard, because restoring the value FOUND is
// what makes the pair correct inside an interrupt handler and correct when
// two nest -- see this file's header.
fn mutex_irq_save() -> usize {
    let flags: usize = (mrs_daif() >> DAIF_IRQ_BIT) & 1;
    disable_irq();
    return flags;
}

// Enables ONLY if the matching save is the one that masked.
fn mutex_irq_restore(flags: usize) {
    if (flags == 0) { enable_irq(); }
}

// Returns the state a matching pool_lock_release must restore: 1 if IRQs
// were already masked when this was called, 0 if they were enabled.
//
// `tag` identifies the pool for the checking backend linux_user
// substitutes here; this one has no use for it, and says so rather than
// dropping it from the signature -- a stand-in whose shape differs from
// the real thing stops catching drift. `lock` is the address of that
// pool's own lock word, which is the half a tag cannot supply.
fn pool_lock_acquire(tag: usize, lock: *Mutex @ id) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return mutex_acquire(lock);
}

// Gives the lock back, then restores what the matching acquire found.
// Enables ONLY if this acquire is the one that masked -- never
// unconditionally, which is the whole difference between this and a bare
// enable_irq().
fn pool_lock_release(tag: usize, lock: *Mutex @ id, flags: usize) !{unsafe} {
    mutex_release(lock, flags);
}
